(BAS-45 - the hang example, LANGUAGE.md:169-178: the blank line ejects
 `increment counter` from the While body, so the condition never changes
 and the loop never terminates - no error, no warning, and `Print "end"`
 never runs.
 The manual's copy prints `inner 1` / `inner 2` forever; this retained
 copy replaces that print with a silent assignment so the hang can be
 recorded as a bounded timeout instead of unbounded output. The manual's
 verbatim version was run separately and does print `inner 1` / `inner 2`
 on repeat until killed.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox BAS-45.vox -o p && timeout 10 ./p
 expected output:
   (exit 124)
)
a number called counter is 1.
While counter is less than or equal to 2,
